In the following file we will briefly document the pseudo mathematical modeling for the TSP. This includes the following:

- Sets & Indices
- Parameters (Data)
- Decision Variables (DV)
- Objective Function (OF)
- Constraints 
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Algorithm: TSP
Goal: Minimize distance
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sets
The set of locations or cities (nodes)
N = {'Location_1', 'Location_2', 'Location_3'..., 'Location_n'}

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Params
d_{i,j}: The distance between nodes 'i','j' that belong to N

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DV:
x_{i,j} ∈ {0,1}
1 if going from Stop i to Stop j happened

Sequence labeling
∀ 'i' ∈ N\{1}:
	u_{i} ∈ Z+

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
OF
MIN (∑_i∈N ∑_{j∈N, j≠i} * (x_{i,j} * d_{i,j}))
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Constraints:
How many times must the salesman leave stop 'i' to go to some stop 'j'
∀ 'i' ∈ N:
	∑_{j∈N, j≠i} * x_{i,j} = 1
	
How many times must the salesman arrive at stop 'i' from some stop 'j'?
∀ 'j' ∈ N:
	∑_{i∈N, i≠j} * x_{i,j} = 1

MTZ Formulation (Miller-Tucker-Zemlin) to avoid subtours
∀ 'i', 'j' ∈ N /{1}, i≠j:
	u_{j} - u_{i} >= 1 - n(1-x_{i,j})


Alternatively we can use the following notation (actually my favorite one): 

∀ 'i'∈ N /{1}:
	∀ 'j'∈ N /{1}, i≠j:
		u_{j} - u_{i} >= 1 - n(1-x_{i,j})

		

